// C3 -- a captured continuation does not carry the parallel spill stack.
//
// Spilled locals used to live inside the frame's own register window; they now
// live on a separate VM-level array, vm->spill_stack, with each CallFrame
// recording its base in `spill_base` and the VM tracking the high-water mark in
// `spill_top`. ObjContinuation predates that move. captureContinuation memcpys
// the CallFrame array -- so each frame's `spill_base` integer rides along as a
// raw field -- and the value stack, but nothing copies the spill VALUES; and
// resumeContinuation restores neither those values nor spill_base/spill_top, so
// the integer that came along means nothing once the VM's spill stack has moved
// on. The two halves show up separately:
//
//   - the values were never snapshotted, so anything that runs on the spill
//     stack while the continuation is suspended overwrites them in place
//     (case 2), and a collection while suspended does not even see them as
//     roots (case 4);
//   - `spill_base` is restored verbatim, so a resumed frame and the next callee
//     pushed above it can be handed the very same spill offsets (cases 1, 3, 5).
//
// This file needs NO environment variable. Every function below declares 260
// locals, past the 252 the default register window allows, so all of them spill
// under a plain `zym c3_continuation_spill_snapshot.zym`. That is the whole
// point: C3 is reachable from ordinary code, not only under a debug knob. Only
// a252..a259 actually land on the spill stack, so only those are read back.
//
// Its companion c3_continuation_spill_snapshot_forced.zym covers the same
// ground cheaply under ZYM_FORCE_SPILL_AT. It has to be a separate file: a
// lowered cap applies to the implicit <script> function too, and top-level
// `var`s and `func` declarations are all locals of it, so a file with this many
// declarations cannot compile in that mode however small its bodies are.
//
// Sums accumulate one term at a time rather than as one long '+' chain, for the
// same reason tests/core/continuations_spill.zym does it: a chain of spilled
// reads needs more scratch registers than SPILL_SCRATCH_COUNT leaves.

var TAG  = Cont.newPrompt("c3")
var TAG2 = Cont.newPrompt("c3-shift")
var HELD = null          // parking slot for the continuation under test

// Resuming through a named global rather than a closure keeps the continuation
// out of an upvalue, which is a different question than the one under test.
func resumeHeld() { return Cont.resume(HELD, 0) }

// A callee whose only job is to occupy spill slots. It is pushed at whatever
